        <h1>What cheap property in Peru looks like</h1>
        <p class="lede">The number in the ad is the start of the cost. I will not invent a national price list. I will describe the object people are actually being offered.</p>
      </div>
    </header>
    <article class="section">
      <div class="wrap measure">
        <p>When I say I can help with an 8×20 meter lot for a few thousand dollars, I mean a kind of lot that exists in some towns, not a promise you will find it behind the park in Miraflores. When I say forest land on the order of 100 hectares, I mean a large rural purchase with more paper, not a softer one. Cheap is a word that hides the object.</p>
        <h2>The small lot</h2>
        <p>It may be titled, urban, and dull, which is the good version: a rectangle, a <span lang="es">partida</span>, parameters that allow a simple house, and a street that already has water and power. It may also be the cheap version of cheap: a possession paper, a slope, a tax debt, or a corner that does not match the drawing. The visit is how you tell those apart. The registry is how your lawyer confirms it.</p>
        <h2>The unfinished shell</h2>
        <p>Brick, a slab, rebar waiting, no windows, a roof that is a promise. People tour it at noon and call it a house. It is a construction project that has paused. Pause can mean money, a family disagreement, or a permit that never existed. <span lang="es">Autoconstrucción</span> is normal in the landscape and it is a different risk from a building an engineer signed. Cracks, columns, and the way the roof meets the wall are the conversation, ideally with an engineer if you intend to sleep under it.</p>
        <h2>The house that still needs a house</h2>
        <p>A place can have a door and still need a new roof, a real electrical install, a cistern, and an elevated tank because municipal pressure is a rumor. In Lima the winter is damp even when it does not rain, and a house without sun and without ventilation grows mold. In the jungle the same sentence adds termites and a hard rain. On the north coast the sentence adds salt and, in a bad year, water where the street was.</p>
        <h2>The large land</h2>
        <p>Hectares look like freedom on a map. On the ground they look like a gate, a track, a neighbor’s track, a ditch, and a question about who has used the place for twenty years. Community land is not a bargain private farm. A <span lang="es">constancia de posesión</span> is not a title you fold into your passport. Water may be a registered right, a custom, or a conflict. If the only access crosses someone else, you have bought a lot you reach by permission until a lawyer tells you otherwise.</p>
        <h2>What the building costs, in kind</h2>
        <p>I will not print a dollar quote and call it the market. Labor from a <span lang="es">maestro de obra</span> is often the line people brag about. Cement, steel, tile, plumbing, a proper electrical job, and a roof that survives the local sky are the lines that move. A single simple room can be a modest local project. A finished house you would hand to a parent is another project. Jungle work adds treatment for insects and for water that comes from the air as well as the sky. Get written quotes on your plan. A number in a Facebook comment is a rumor with confidence.</p>
        <h2>Holding is allowed. It is not free.</h2>
        <p>You can buy land and build years later. You can also sell. I have sold for a profit because the purchase price fit what I knew of the range. That is not a forecast for you. While the land sits, someone should look at it. Predial still exists for titled property. A caretaker is a person, not a concept. Values can fall.</p>
        <p>If this is the essay that made the ad look different, the next useful page is <a href="../start.html">what you want</a>, and then a rental before a deed. I can help with the rental.</p>
      </div>
    </article>
